= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the <numeralwise enumeration in arithmetic> convention in the <first-order theory>: membership supplies a standard stage with a provable enumerating instance, while nonmembership makes every standard-stage instance refutable. Put $D=\{n:P\vdash E_n(\bar n)\}$. By the definition of $a$, the diagonal instance $E_a(\bar a)$ is exactly $G$.

If $P\vdash G$, then $a\in D$. <Numeralwise enumeration in arithmetic> supplies a <natural number> $m$ such that $P\vdash A(\bar a,\bar m)$. But universal instantiation of $G$ gives $P\vdash\neg A(\bar a,\bar m)$. This contradicts <first-order consistency>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{P\text{ consistent}\Longrightarrow P\nvdash G.}
$$
This uses the <formal proof> obligations in <numeralwise enumeration in arithmetic> in $P$, rather than merely the external assertion that a <logical formula> has a particular complexity.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

<One-consistency> says that no <bounded arithmetic formula> $F(x)$ has a provable existential closure while each of its standard numeral instances is refutable. It implies <first-order consistency>, since an inconsistent classical <first-order theory> proves every <logical formula>.

If $P$ is <one-consistent>, (i) gives $P\nvdash G$, so $a\notin D$. <Numeralwise enumeration in arithmetic> therefore gives
$$
P\vdash\neg A(\bar a,\bar m)\qquad\text{for every standard }m.
$$
If $P\vdash\neg G$, classical logic gives $P\vdash\exists v_2 A(\bar a,v_2)$. The matrix is bounded, and the preceding displayed derivations refute every standard instance. This violates <one-consistency>. Consequently
$$
\boxed{P\text{ one-consistent}\Longrightarrow P\nvdash\neg G.}
$$
Together with (i), this yields undecidability of this <first-order sentence> under the stronger hypothesis, without asserting that ordinary <first-order consistency> alone suffices for its negative half.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

<Bounded completeness of an arithmetic theory> means that every bounded <arithmetic> <first-order sentence> true in the standard <natural numbers> is provable in $S$. If $S$ is <first-order consistent> and proves a false <bounded arithmetic sentence> $B$, <bounded completeness of an arithmetic theory> also proves its true bounded <negation>, a <contradiction>. Conversely, if every bounded theorem is true, $S$ cannot be inconsistent, because inconsistency would prove the false <bounded arithmetic sentence> $0=1$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Con}(S)\iff\Sigma_0\text{-soundness}.}
$$
If $S$ is <one-consistent> and proves $\exists xB(x)$ with bounded matrix, that <existential sentence> cannot be false: otherwise every $\neg B(\bar n)$ would be a true <bounded arithmetic sentence> and hence provable, contrary to <one-consistency>. Thus $S$ has <Sigma-1 soundness>.

Conversely, <Sigma-1 soundness> excludes inconsistency. If $S\vdash\exists xB(x)$, it supplies a standard witness $n$ with $B(n)$ true. <Bounded completeness of an arithmetic theory> proves $B(\bar n)$, so <first-order consistency> forbids a <formal proof> of $\neg B(\bar n)$. Hence there cannot simultaneously be refutations of all standard instances. Therefore
$$
\boxed{1\text{-consistency}\iff\Sigma_1\text{-soundness}.}
$$
Finite blocks of existential <logical variables> are treated as finite <tuples> of witnesses, with the same argument.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

<Omega-consistency> forbids a <logical formula> $B(x)$ of any complexity for which $S\vdash\exists xB(x)$ and $S\vdash\neg B(\bar n)$ for every standard $n$. The difference from <one-consistency> is that the matrix is unrestricted. It implies <first-order consistency>.

For a complete <first-order consistent> classical <first-order theory>, theorem membership obeys the ordinary Boolean <truth tables>. For example, it proves a <conjunction> exactly when it proves both conjuncts; completeness and <first-order consistency> also make theoremhood of a <negation> the complement of theoremhood of the <first-order sentence>. <Omega-consistency> additionally gives the witness property
$$
S\vdash\exists xB(x)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
S\vdash B(\bar n)\text{ for some standard }n.
$$
The reverse implication is existential introduction. For the forward implication, if no standard instance were provable, completeness would give a <formal proof> of each negated instance, violating <omega-consistency>. This works also after replacing additional <free variables> by numerals.

Now induct on <logical formulas> with their remaining <free variables> replaced by standard numerals. At the atomic level, the equivalence between theoremhood and standard truth follows from <bounded completeness of an arithmetic theory> and bounded <soundness of an arithmetic theory> from (b). The Boolean steps use the truth-table facts just proved. The existential step uses the witness property and the <induction> hypothesis on its matrix; <universal quantification> follows by <negation>. Hence
$$
\boxed{S\vdash X\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\mathbb N\models X}
$$
for every <first-order sentence> $X$. Completeness ensures it decides each <first-order sentence>, and this equivalence ensures every decision is correct.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Start with the supplied bounded-complete, <one-consistent> <first-order theory> $T$ proving a false <first-order sentence> $F$. Replace it by its deductive closure, which preserves these properties and contains $F$. Enumerate the <arithmetic> <first-order sentences> as $X_0,X_1,\ldots$. Recursively extend the current <first-order theory> by either $X_n$ or $\neg X_n$, choosing an extension which remains <one-consistent> as permitted by the supplied lemma, and then take its deductive closure. Call the resulting increasing sequence $T_n$, with $T_0$ the initial <first-order theory>, and put $S=\bigcup_nT_n$.

Every stage is still bounded-complete. By (b), every stage has <Sigma-1 soundness>. If $S$ proved a false Sigma-1 <first-order sentence>, the finite <formal proof> would use assumptions from one stage $T_n$, contradicting its <Sigma-1 soundness>. Thus $S$ has <Sigma-1 soundness> and is bounded-complete, so (b) makes it <one-consistent>.

This <soundness of an arithmetic theory> argument is essential: the definition of <one-consistency> involves infinitely many standard refutations, so it would be insufficient merely to say that a violation has a finite <formal proof>. What has a finite <formal proof> is the false <existential sentence>, and <bounded completeness of an arithmetic theory> is what turns <one-consistency> into the needed <soundness of an arithmetic theory> condition.

The <union> is deductively closed because each <formal proof> has finitely many assumptions. It contains either $X$ or $\neg X$ for every <first-order sentence>, by the <numeralwise enumeration in arithmetic>, and contains the false <first-order sentence> $F$. If it were omega-consistent, (c) would make it correct on all <first-order sentences>, contradicting $F$. We have therefore constructed
$$
\boxed{S\text{ complete and one-consistent, but not omega-consistent}.}
$$
<Omega-consistency> always implies <one-consistency> by restricting its matrices. This counterexample proves that the converse is strictly false.

