A <logical formula> separates $A$ from $B$ in $S$ if it is provable at the standard numeral <tuple> of every member of $A$ and refutable at that of every member of $B$:
$$
\vec n\in A\Longrightarrow S\vdash F(\bar{\vec n}),\qquad
\vec n\in B\Longrightarrow S\vdash\neg F(\bar{\vec n}).
$$
There is no obligation on <tuples> outside the two <sets>. In a <first-order consistent> <first-order theory> these two obligations make $A$ and $B$ disjoint.

Let $h$ be the <Gödel number> of $H(v_1)$ and take the literal diagonal <first-order sentence> $R=H(\bar h)$. If $S\vdash R$, then $h$ belongs to the second source <set>, so separation gives $S\vdash\neg H(\bar h)$, a <contradiction>. If $S\vdash\neg R$, then $h$ belongs to the first source <set>, so separation gives $S\vdash H(\bar h)$, again a <contradiction>. Hence
$$
\boxed{S\text{ consistent}\Longrightarrow S\nvdash R\text{ and }S\nvdash\neg R.}
$$
The direction of separation matters: here $H$ is provable on refutable diagonal codes and refutable on provable diagonal codes.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $H(\vec v)$ for the proposed stage-comparison <logical formula> and fix a standard <tuple> $\vec a$. If $\vec a\in A$, <numeralwise enumeration in arithmetic> supplies a standard $m$ with $S\vdash F(\bar{\vec a},\bar m)$. Disjointness gives $\vec a\notin B$, so $S\vdash\neg G(\bar{\vec a},\bar j)$ for every standard $j$, in particular $j=0,\ldots,m$. The source's finite initial-segment axiom combines this finite list into
$$
S\vdash\forall y\le\bar m\,\neg G(\bar{\vec a},y).
$$
Use $\bar m$ as the <existential witness> to obtain $S\vdash H(\bar{\vec a})$.

If instead $\vec a\in B$, choose $n$ with $S\vdash G(\bar{\vec a},\bar n)$. Disjointness gives refutations of $F(\bar{\vec a},\bar j)$ for all $j$, so the finitely many $j\le n$ give
$$
S\vdash\forall x\le\bar n\,\neg F(\bar{\vec a},x).
$$
For an arbitrary $x$, the source's comparison axiom gives $x\le\bar n$ or $\bar n\le x$. In the first case the first conjunct of the proposed witness condition is false. In the second case $y=\bar n$ witnesses failure of its bounded universal conjunct, since $G(\bar{\vec a},\bar n)$ is provable. Generalization over $x$ therefore gives $S\vdash\neg H(\bar{\vec a})$.

Thus **the <logical formula> separates $A$ from $B$**. The <formal proof> uses the enumerators' numeralwise positive and negative <formal proof> properties and the two explicit order principles supplied in the source; it does not assume the <first-order theory> can decide arbitrary unbounded existential <logical formulas>.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A <logical formula> $D(\vec x,y)$ weakly defines a total numerical <function> $f$ in $S$ if, for every standard input <tuple> $\vec n$ and $m=f(\vec n)$,
$$
S\vdash D(\bar{\vec n},\bar m),\qquad
S\vdash\neg D(\bar{\vec n},\bar r)\quad(r\ne m).
$$
<Strong definability of a numerical function> additionally requires the internal uniqueness statement
$$
S\vdash\forall y\,(D(\bar{\vec n},y)\to y=\bar m).
$$
Together with the positive instance, this is equivalent to proving $\forall y(D(\bar{\vec n},y)\leftrightarrow y=\bar m)$ for each standard input. The <universal quantifier> is the extra strength: it covers nonstandard potential outputs in <first-order models> of $S$. These are the weak and strong <function> conventions used here. https://www.brucewatkins.org/math/docs/Smullyan.pdf[Gödel's Incompleteness Theorems, printed page99, definition of weak and <strong definability of a numerical function> of <functions>]{ref}

Suppose the graph of the total <function> is Sigma-1. Its complement is also Sigma-1, since
$$
f(\vec x)\ne y\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\exists z\,(f(\vec x)=z\wedge z\ne y).
$$
Replace the graph assertion on the right by its Sigma-1 definition; <existential quantifiers> can be collected into one block. <Bounded completeness of an arithmetic theory> supplies numeralwise stage enumerators for each of these two disjoint complementary <sets>. Apply (b) to them. Its separating <logical formula> is provable exactly at the required graph <tuples> in the positive direction and refutable at all the other standard <tuples>, proving <weak definability of a numerical function>.

Using the graph <logical formula> alone would only supply positive instances from true existential witnesses; the separation construction is what supplies the negative-instance <formal proofs> without assuming <Sigma-1 soundness> or completeness for false Sigma-1 statements.

= d
