Write $\Box A$ for $P(\ulcorner A\urcorner)$. A standard <arithmetical provability predicate> obeys the <Hilbert-Bernays-Löb derivability conditions>:
$$
S\vdash A\Longrightarrow S\vdash\Box A,\qquad
S\vdash\Box(A\to B)\to(\Box A\to\Box B),\qquad
S\vdash\Box A\to\Box\Box A.
$$
The first condition is a rule about theorems; the latter two are statements proved within the <first-order theory>. When the terminology also includes external adequacy, require that $P(\bar n)$ be true in the standard <natural numbers> exactly when the <logical formula> with code $n$ has an $S$-proof.

For <Peano arithmetic>, code finite strings, <logical formulas> and <formal proof> sequences by <natural numbers>. <Formal proof> checking is effective and has an arithmetized bounded checking relation with its computation data. Existentially assert the existence of a correct <formal proof> code and any required checking data. The resulting <predicate symbol> is Sigma-1, and its standard truth means exactly that an actual finite PA <formal proof> exists. Effective operations on <formal proof> codes combine an implication <formal proof> with an antecedent <formal proof>, and turn a <formal proof> into a <formal proof> that its code is a <formal proof>; PA verifies those constructions. They give distribution and <positive introspection>, while an actual theorem's finite <formal proof> gives its internal provability assertion. This is the requested rough construction, with the three internal <formal proof> properties distinguished from external agreement.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Abbreviate $P(\ulcorner A\urcorner)$ by $\Box A$. Since $S\vdash\neg X$, it proves $X\to G$. The theoremhood and distribution conditions give
$$
S\vdash\Box X\to\Box G.
$$
The fixed-point equation gives $S\vdash G\to(\Box G\to X)$, because $G$ implies $\neg\Box G$ and a false antecedent implies any conclusion. Applying theoremhood and distribution yields
$$
S\vdash\Box G\to\Box(\Box G\to X).
$$
<Positive introspection> gives $\Box G\to\Box\Box G$; distribution then gives $\Box G\to\Box X$. Thus $S\vdash\Box G\leftrightarrow\Box X$. Combining with $G\leftrightarrow\neg\Box G$ proves
$$
\boxed{S\vdash G\leftrightarrow\neg\Box X.}
$$
In particular, choosing a fixed logical <contradiction> for $X$ identifies this <Gödel sentence> with the <formal consistency statement> of $S$.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

If $S\vdash G$, theoremhood gives $S\vdash\Box G$, while the fixed-point equivalence also gives $S\vdash\neg\Box G$. Therefore <first-order consistency> implies $S\nvdash G$.

If $S\cup\{\neg G\}$ were inconsistent, the deduction theorem and <classical propositional logic> would give $S\vdash\neg\neg G$, hence $S\vdash G$. This is impossible by the preceding argument. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Con}(S)\Longrightarrow\operatorname{Con}(S+\neg G).}
$$
This is a syntactic <first-order consistency> result. It does not claim that the extra axiom is true in the standard <natural numbers> or that the extended <first-order theory> is <sound>.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

First establish the transfer of provability requested in the hint. The propositional theorem $X\to(\neg G\to X)$ and the <derivability conditions> give
$$
S\vdash\Box X\to\Box(\neg G\to X).
$$
The supplied arithmetized deduction equivalence identifies the right-hand provability assertion, within $S^*$, with $\Box^*X$. Hence
$$
S^*\vdash\Box X\to\Box^*X.
$$
Choose a fixed <contradiction> $\bot$ for $X$. Part (b) gives $S\vdash G\leftrightarrow\neg\Box\bot$, so adjoining $\neg G$ gives $S^*\vdash\Box\bot$. The transfer implication now gives $S^*\vdash\Box^*\bot$.

Apply (b) inside $S^*$ to its own <Gödel sentence>: it proves $G^*\leftrightarrow\neg\Box^*\bot$. The last provability assertion therefore entails
$$
\boxed{S^*\vdash\neg G^*.}
$$
Thus the extension proves the <negation> of its own <Gödel sentence> as well. When $S$ is <first-order consistent>, (c) says this phenomenon occurs in a <first-order consistent> extension; the extension asserts an internal <formal proof> of <contradiction> without there being an actual standard <formal proof> of <contradiction>.

